parts.text:
The `tagalign` tool incorrectly strips or empties values in Go struct tags during the alignment and fixing process. For example, when processing a struct like:

type Foo struct {
	Foo []string `json:"foo,omitempty" long:"foo" env:"FOO" env-delim:"," description:"List of foos."`
}

The tool outputs:

type Foo struct {
	Foo []string `json:"foo,omitempty" long:"foo" env:"FOO" description:"List of foos." env-delim:""`
}

Notice that the value `,` for the `env-delim` tag has been completely removed, resulting in `env-delim:""`.

This occurs because the underlying struct tag parser over-parses tag values, interpreting internal characters (like commas) as delimiters or options, which corrupts the original tag data. The struct tag handler should treat tag values as opaque strings, preserving all characters exactly as they appear in the source code, without attempting to split or re-parse internal options within the value itself. Ensure that all struct tag values are preserved intact during alignment, sorting, and strict mode operations.

