The `requiredfield` tool panics with a nil pointer dereference when analyzing Go code containing nested anonymous functions (func literals) that return struct fields. This occurs in `isReturnedWithNonNilError` because the function walks up the AST from a return statement to find the matching enclosing function type, but fails to break when it finds the closest function type. As a result, it continues past the innermost function definition and uses the wrong signature or a nil type, causing a crash.

For example, analyzing this code triggers the panic:
```go
type Params struct {
    Name string // required
}

func foo() {
    f := func() string {
        return Params{}.Name
    }
    fmt.Println(f())
}
```

The logic in `isReturnedWithNonNilError` needs to correctly identify the innermost enclosing function type when traversing up from a return statement. It must break out of the traversal loop immediately upon finding the nearest `*ast.FuncLit` or `*ast.FuncDecl` to avoid resolving to an outer scope or nil. Refactoring this resolution into a dedicated helper that cleanly handles the break condition will prevent the incorrect type assignment and subsequent nil pointer dereference.

